{"actor":"Joshua Oliphant","comment":null,"created_at":"2026-04-10T17:50:29Z","event_type":"status_changed","id":44,"issue_id":"brooklet-3ot","new_value":"{\"status\":\"in_progress\"}","old_value":"{\"id\":\"brooklet-3ot\",\"title\":\"CLI: brooklet watch command for Monitor-compatible topic tailing\",\"description\":\"Add a new 'brooklet watch \\u003ctopic\\u003e' CLI command that tails a brooklet topic and emits one compact human-readable line per event (e.g. '#42 14:03:22 type=ping n=10'), suitable for use with Claude Code's Monitor tool which turns each stdout line into a chat notification.\\n\\nRationale: brooklet's follow-mode consumer with offset tracking is a near-perfect fit for Monitor — resumable across restarts, no missed events, no replay. Existing 'consume --follow' emits raw JSON envelopes which would be too noisy for Monitor (it auto-kills verbose streams).\\n\\nPlan: /Users/joshuaoliphant/.claude/plans/enumerated-orbiting-boot.md\\n\\nScope:\\n- New 'brooklet watch \\u003ctopic\\u003e' core CLI command (cli.py)\\n- New pure formatter module src/brooklet/watch_format.py\\n- Line-buffered stdout for pipe compatibility (sys.stdout.reconfigure)\\n- Default group 'watch' for resumability\\n- Tests: tests/test_watch_format.py (10 unit tests), tests/test_watch.py (CliRunner + 1 subprocess smoke test for real buffering)\\n- TDD: tests first\\n\\nOut of scope: per-adapter scout/pytest formatters, --filter flag, fixing existing missing-file-in-follow-mode limitation.\",\"status\":\"open\",\"priority\":2,\"issue_type\":\"feature\",\"owner\":\"joshua.oliphant@hey.com\",\"created_at\":\"2026-04-11T00:50:24Z\",\"created_by\":\"Joshua Oliphant\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-04-11T00:50:24Z\"}"}
